  section: "Hiring & Staffing",
};

const topics = ["careers", "compliance", "staffing"];

const faqItems = [
  { question: "Do you need a license or permit to work at a Maine dispensary?", answer: "Maine does not issue a generic 'worker permit.' Instead, most people who work in or for a licensed adult-use cannabis establishment need an Individual Identification Card (IIC) from the Office of Cannabis Policy (OCP). Whether you need one depends on your duties, system access, and authority — not your job title. Anyone who possesses, cultivates, manufactures, packages, tests, dispenses, transfers, serves, handles, transports, or delivers cannabis, or who can access or enter data in the inventory tracking or point-of-sale system, must hold a valid IIC. So do all principals (officers, directors, managers, general partners)." },
  { question: "How old do you have to be to work at a dispensary in Maine?", answer: "You must be at least 21 years old to hold an employment Individual Identification Card (IIC) under 18-691 C.M.R. ch. 20. This applies to budtenders and other plant-touching or system-access roles. The 21+ threshold mirrors the adult-use purchase age." },
  { question: "How do you get a Maine cannabis IIC?", answer: "Apply through OCP's online IIC application portal (InforME / licensing.web.maine.gov). You submit the IIC application and required information, pay the application fee, and complete a fingerprint-based criminal history check (typically scheduled through IdentoGO). OCP reviews the application; applications with outstanding requirements not completed within 90 days of submission are considered abandoned. Your employer (the licensee) sponsors the role, but the card is issued to you by OCP." },
  { question: "What disqualifies you from getting a Maine cannabis IIC?", answer: "A 'disqualifying drug offense' bars a person from acting as a principal of a licensed establishment, though OCP may still issue that person an employment IIC (18-691 C.M.R. ch. 20 §1(2)(D)). It generally means a controlled-substance conviction punishable by one year or more in prison — unless the sentence, including any probation, incarceration, or supervised release, was completed five or more years before the application, or the conduct is now authorized under Maine's cannabis laws (Title 28-B). OCP evaluates applications individually; a past conviction does not automatically disqualify every applicant. Principals must submit to a fingerprint-based criminal history check every two years." },
  { question: "How long is a Maine IIC valid?", answer: "An Individual Identification Card is valid for two years from the date of issue under 18-691 C.M.R. ch. 20. You must renew before it expires to keep working in a plant-touching or system-access role. Active IIC holders can request a replacement card through OCP's online portal if the card is lost." },
  { question: "Do you need a medical card to work at a dispensary in Maine?", answer: "No. A medical cannabis patient registration is not required to work at a Maine dispensary. What most employees need is the employment Individual Identification Card (IIC), which is separate from any medical patient card. The medical program (registry identification cards under Title 22) governs patients and caregivers, not adult-use employment." },
  { question: "Does Maine require a responsible-vendor training course for dispensary employees?", answer: "Maine's OCP licensing rules and IIC instructions do not impose a universal state-mandated 'responsible vendor' training course, annual certification, or manager endorsement for every cannabis employee. That said, the licensee still has a duty to train staff for their actual roles — age verification, inventory tracking, security, sanitation, and incident response where relevant. Requirements can change, so confirm current training expectations with your employer and OCP." },
  { question: "What jobs are available at a Maine dispensary?", answer: "Common dispensary roles include budtender (sales and customer consultation), inventory and compliance staff, cultivation and processing roles (for vertically integrated operators), management, and security. Pay varies by role and region. For a detailed budtender career guide including 2026 pay ranges and the interview process, see the Maine Budtender Careers guide." },
];
